  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1040 PM MDT Wed Jun 10 2026\n\nThe northern stream continues to dominate the weather pattern\nacross NOAM with a trailing wave moving across the Northern\nRockies to the Northern Plains over through the next 24 hours.\nThis wave is forcing a dry cold front southward which has\narrived to far northeast Utah this evening. With moisture in the\n10th percentile and only moderate lapse rates this front moves\nthrough uneventful overnight except for gusty winds marking the\nfropa. As usual post frontal winds will be the strongest over\nthe southern valleys into the Gunnison Basin and this is where\ncritical fire weather conditions persist tomorrow. The remainder\nof the area will get a short reprieve with these conditions more\nlocalized. Temperatures will remain near to slightly below\nnormal across all but the far southern zones tomorrow. This\nchanges on Friday as zonal flow increases and temperatures\nreturn to above normal across the board by 5 to 10 degrees.\nZonal flow and warmer temperatures lead to deep mixing and a\nreturn of widespread windy conditions and likely another round\nof critical fire weather. The SubTropical High will be scooting\neastward under the zonal flow on Saturday and this looks to\nforce elevated moisture northward into the 4 Corners region and\nlead to at least isolated afternoon mountain convection. Not\nliking the early Sunday forecast of elevated moisture lingering\nand an increasing jet aloft as the pattern tightens aloft. We\nreally don't need another round of nocturnal lightning and this\nwill be something to monitor. Eyes turn to mountain showers and\nthunderstorms this weekend. Additional moisture advects \nnorthward on Sunday, bringing additional cloud cover and better \nchances for afternoon showers. Dry boundary layer conditions \ncould make a tough time for measurable rain though. This will \nknock high temperatures down a few degrees on Sunday and Monday.\nTuesday, high pressure builds back over the West, likely \nsending temperatures well above average heading towards the \nSolstice. This likely increases the chances we remain in a \ncritical fire weather pattern heading into next week.",
